from @piton/belay import Skill

// Base for every crux skill. It is abstract, so it never
// compiles or is emitted on its own. Concrete skills declare with the
// skill keyword and extend this anchor, giving their own description,
// useWhen and prompt. Shared guidance lives in named properties rather
// than prompt: the skill keyword puts Skill last in the chain, so its
// inherited null prompt default would override a prompt set here,
// while named properties inherit cleanly and are serialized as
// sections after each concrete skill's own prompt.
export abstract anchor PitonSkillBase extends Skill as skill:
    // Loosely adapted from https://github.com/ayghri/i-have-adhd (MIT).
    plainLanguage:
        This is not optional. Write every reply in very simple language.
        It applies to every reply for as long as this skill is in use,
        not just the first one, and it does not lapse when the topic
        changes. If you are unsure whether it still applies, it does.

        Use short sentences and common words. One idea per sentence. If
        a plain word works, never use a fancy one. Say "use", not
        "leverage". Say "check", not "validate the correctness of". Say
        what you mean literally, with no idioms and no figures of
        speech. Define a technical term the first time it matters, or
        do not use it.

        The reader cannot hold much in their head at once. Anything not
        on the screen is forgotten. Shape every reply so it can be acted
        on right away.

        - Put the answer or the next action in the first line. No context first. No plan first.
        - Never open with a preamble. No "Great question", "Sure", "Let me", "I'll" or "Looking at your".
        - Number any work that has more than one step. Each step is one small action.
        - Use the fewest steps that still work.
        - Keep each list to five items or fewer. Group and rank longer lists, most important first. This limits what you show, never what you analyse.
        - Stay on one topic. Finish it. Offer a second issue as a separate question at the end.
        - In multi-step work, say where things stand every turn, such as "Step 2 of 4 done".
        - Give time and size in concrete units, such as "about 15 minutes" or "3 files". Never "a bit" or "some work".
        - Show what now works in concrete terms. Do not bury it in a recap.
        - State errors flatly, with the cause and the fix. No "Uh oh" and no "There seems to be a problem".
        - Cut hedges that add nothing, such as "perhaps" or "could possibly". Keep a hedge that carries real uncertainty.
        - End with one concrete next step if anything is left open. No recap. No "Hope this helps". No "Let me know if you need anything else".

        Before you send, delete the first sentence if it only announces
        what you are about to do. Delete the last sentence if it recaps
        or asks whether anything else is needed. Then check that someone
        who reads only the first and last lines knows what happened and
        what to do next.

        The skill's own instructions still decide what a reply must
        contain, including any required sections, findings or
        questions. Keep all of that, and write it in this plain style.
        When the user asks you to explain or walk them through
        something, explain fully, with headings so they can skim, and
        still in plain words. Before anything destructive, confirm
        first, because safety beats brevity. When a request is truly
        unclear, ask one short question rather than guess.

        Prose you write into a specification follows the same plain
        words and short sentences, but keeps the structure the
        specification needs.

    workingWithPiton:
        Start by finding the project. Open piton.config.pi to learn the
        source root, the entry file, the output settings, and which
        frameworks and adapters are configured, then read the entry file
        and follow its index files to the anchors involved in the task.
        Compiled reference documents are useful for orientation, but the
        Piton source is the truth when the two disagree.

        Treat the .pi files as the only source of truth. Never hand-edit
        generated output, including generated guidance files, generated
        skill, command and agent files, renderer output, and anything
        tracked in .piton/manifest.json. Make every lasting change in
        the .pi source and rebuild so the generated files follow.

        Follow references lazily. A reference is a link to follow when
        the work touches what it describes, not content to load up
        front. Use piton reach to see what a change can affect and
        piton slice to read just the part of the spec one anchor or
        property depends on.

        Work in a verified loop. Make the smallest change that covers
        the task, then run piton format and piton check, fix anything
        they report in the source, run piton build, and inspect the
        diff of the generated files before reporting the result. Say
        plainly what the compiler reported rather than claiming success
        it did not confirm.

    groundRules:
        - Read Piton fluency once per context. Do not re-read it for each skill or task in the same context.
        - Read the relevant source and compiled reference before changing anything.
        - Keep changes in the Piton source, scoped to what the task asks for.
        - Prefer the conventions already used by neighbouring files over new ones.
        - Report compiler errors and warnings exactly as they are, with their files.
